= Balanced Scorecard and ICT project Management
== Balanced Scorecard (BSC)

A Balanced Scorecard (BSC) is a performance measurement tool used to identify, improve, and control different functions and outcomes of a business. It acts as a strategy map that helps balance the needs of today and the future. Originally developed for for-profit companies, it was later adapted for nonprofit organizations and government agencies.

=== Four Main Aspects (Perspectives) of BSC
+ *Learning and Growth:* Analyzed by examining employee training, skills, knowledge, and available resources. It focuses on how well information and knowledge are captured and how effectively employees use them to create a competitive advantage.
+ *Business Processes:* Evaluated by examining how efficiently products or services are produced and delivered. Operational management is analyzed to identify gaps, delays, bottlenecks, shortages, and waste.
+ *Customer Perspective:* Used to measure customer satisfaction with the quality, price, and availability of products or services based on customer feedback.
+ *Financial Data:* Uses financial metrics such as sales, expenses, income, profits, financial ratios, budget variances, and income targets to understand financial performance.

=== Main Role of Balanced Scorecard
- Helps develop performance measures, set objectives, and collect and analyze data from each of the four perspectives.
- Provides an effective method for measuring and analyzing the implementation of a plan to ensure better performance and quality of the project.

=== Steps for BSC
+ Establish a clear vision of the future.
+ Define strategic objectives.
+ Determine critical success factors and resources.
+ Choose indicators to measure and monitor performance.
+ Set goals, action plans, and initiatives.

== COCOMO Model (Constructive Cost Model)

COCOMO is a software cost estimation model used to estimate the effort, development time, and cost of a software project based on its size (measured in Lines of Code -- LOC, or Thousands of Lines of Code -- KLOC/KDLOC).

=== Steps of the COCOMO Model
+ *Estimate Software Size:* Estimate the size of the software in thousands of lines of code (KLOC).
+ *Calculate Initial Effort:* Use the estimated software size to calculate the initial development effort.
+ *Determine Multiplying Factors:* Consider project factors such as product complexity, team experience, tools, and working environment.
+ *Calculate Final Effort:* Adjust the initial effort using the multiplying factors to obtain the final effort estimate.

=== Types of Projects in COCOMO
+ *Organic:*
  - Small and simple projects where the problem is well understood.
  - Developed by a small team with reasonable experience.
  - *Example:* Simple business or inventory management software.
+ *Semi-Detached:*
  - Medium complexity projects with moderate team size and varied experience.
  - Moderately complex programming environments.
+ *Embedded:*
  - High complexity projects with strict requirements and constraints.
  - Requires a large, highly skilled, and experienced team.
  - *Example:* Aircraft control or medical control systems.

=== Types of COCOMO Models
+ *Basic COCOMO Model:* Estimates effort mainly using the size of the software (KLOC).
+ *Intermediate COCOMO Model:* Uses software size plus cost drivers / multiplying factors.
+ *Detailed COCOMO Model:* Provides a more detailed estimation by considering different phases of the software development process.


== Six Sigma

Six Sigma is a step-by-step, data-driven method used to improve product and process quality by finding and removing the root causes of errors and defects.
- *Main Goal:* To make processes so reliable that they produce almost zero defects (targeting no more than 3.4 errors per million opportunities).
- *How It Works:* It relies on two main approaches: *DMAIC* to fix and improve existing processes, and *DMADV* to design brand-new processes or products from scratch.
- *Key Focus:* Instead of relying on guesswork, it uses facts, numbers, and statistical tools to measure performance and solve problems permanently.

== Pareto Analysis & 80/20 Rule

Pareto Analysis is a simple problem-solving tool based on the famous *80/20 rule*, which states that a large majority of problems come from a small number of key causes.
- *The 80/20 Rule:* For example, about 80% of project delays or software bugs usually come from just 20% of the root causes.
- *How It Is Shown:* It uses a *Pareto Chart*—a special graph that combines bars (showing individual problems from highest to lowest) with a line (showing cumulative totals).
- *Why It Matters:* It helps project managers quickly spot the "vital few" problems so they know exactly where to focus their time, money, and effort to get the best results.